## Content, Compliance and Who Writes It

Healthcare content should be authored or reviewed by a qualified practitioner with relevant expertise. I provide search research, page structures, content briefs, internal linking plans, schema requirements and technical implementation guidance. The clinician supplies or signs off the clinical substance.

That division is deliberate. I can show which question a page needs to answer and how supporting information should be organised.

I cannot decide whether a statement about a condition, procedure, suitability or risk is clinically correct. The named practitioner and provider own that decision.

Advertising compliance also stays with the provider. ASA and CAP requirements plus your professional regulator's guidance (GMC, GDC, HCPC or NMC as relevant) sit within your compliance team's remit.

I build the search and content system around the approvals, wording and evidence your team provides. I don't reinterpret those standards as medical or legal advice.

The publishing workflow should record who wrote the page, who reviewed it and when that review happened. Each date must describe a real event. A site-wide footer date isn't evidence that every clinical page was reviewed on that date.

## Healthcare SEO Questions

**Does Google treat healthcare websites differently?**

Yes. YMYL pages need demonstrable expertise, clear author credentials and accurate content that qualified practitioners have authored or reviewed.

**Who writes the medical content?**

Your clinicians author or review the medical content. I provide the search structure, briefs, schema and technical work while the provider retains responsibility for clinical accuracy and approval.

**How do you measure healthcare SEO?**

I measure healthcare SEO through enquiries, calls, bookings and visibility for service and location terms. I never use clinical outcomes as an SEO performance measure.

